<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Transport security for everything Android's own networking stack carries (Task 62, §7).

  ## Cleartext is off by default, and there is still no `debug-overrides` block

  A debug override is how a permissive trust configuration reaches production: it is added
  to unblock one afternoon's testing against a self-signed PBX, and nothing ever removes
  it. There is none here and the CI gate refuses one. A deployment that needs to trust its
  own certificate authority configures one explicitly - see StackAccount.customCaPath -
  which ADDS a trust anchor rather than disabling validation.

  ## The one exception, and why it is this shape

  This file used to say "no per-domain exception" as well. That stopped being true when the
  Coral UC platform had to be reached at `http://192.168.250.201`, a lab deployment on a
  private network whose TLS certificate does not name the address it is served on.

  Three routes were possible and two are worse:

   - A permissive `X509TrustManager` over https. Forbidden outright by section 7 and by the
     "Assert no permissive TrustManager" gate, and rightly: it disables validation for
     every host, not one, and it is invisible at the call site.
   - `cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"` on the base config. That removes the protection
     from every request the app makes, to reach one server.
   - What is below: cleartext for **named private-network literals only**.

  The exception is narrow in a way a reader can check, and it is ENFORCED rather than
  merely intended - the "Assert cleartext traffic is refused" gate now also asserts that
  every domain in this block is an RFC 1918 address. A public hostname cannot be added to
  it without the build failing, which is the property that makes a per-domain exception
  survivable at all.

  The base config is unchanged: everything not named below still refuses cleartext.

  ## What this does and does not cover

  It covers Firebase and any HTTP this app makes through the platform.

  It does NOT cover SIP over TLS: PJSIP opens its own sockets and runs its own TLS
  through OpenSSL, so `network_security_config.xml` never sees them. The equivalent
  settings for that path belong to the TLS transport RealPjsipCoreGateway creates, which
  verifies the server certificate against the bundle PjsipTrustStore assembles from the
  device's own trust store. Both halves are needed and neither substitutes for the other;
  docs/security.md says so in one place rather than leaving somebody to discover it. What
  has not happened yet is a TLS registration against a real server - docs/pjsip-migration.md
  P-8 is where that is tracked.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!-- The system store, and nothing else. -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <!--
      Lab deployments on private networks, reached over plain HTTP.

      RFC 1918 literals only. The CI gate checks every entry here against that, so this
      list cannot grow a public hostname without failing the build.
    -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <!-- The Coral UC platform under test (services-app). -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">192.168.250.201</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
